<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=tire">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pneumatic tyre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1845">1845</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Tire</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A tyre is a ring-shaped component that surrounds a wheel's rim, carries the vehicle's load to the ground and provides traction. Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>, tyres were metal bands fitted around wooden wheels. The first patent for what appears to be a standard pneumatic tyre was lodged in 1845 by the Scottish inventor Robert William Thomson, but the tyres were expensive and not a commercial success. His second patent of 1867 covered solid India-rubber tyres. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-boyd-dunlop">John Boyd Dunlop</a> made the first successful pneumatic tyre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belfast">Belfast</a> in 1888, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelin">Michelin</a> made the first pneumatic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/automobile">automobile</a> tyres in 1895.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tire.jpg" alt="Pneumatic tyre" width="330" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anselm Schüler · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Large_tyre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1845</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>London, England</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Robert William Thomson · <a href="/encyclopedia/john-boyd-dunlop">John Boyd Dunlop</a> · <a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Dunlop's 1888 tyre is often given as the first, but in 1892 his patent was declared invalid because of Thomson's earlier patents (London 1845, France 1846, USA 1847).</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/wheel">Wheel<span>before 4000 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">Rubber<span>c. 1600 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/vulcanization">Vulcanization<span>1839</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=tire">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
